MASTER PROMPT: EVIDENCE-INFORMED DE-AI-FICATION AND ART-DIRECTED REDESIGN OF AN EXISTING WEBSITE
1. YOUR ROLE AND MISSION
You are acting as a combined website art director, senior product designer, design-systems architect, front-end engineering lead, content strategist, and QA engineer.

. Your mission is to carry out a complete, implemented, verified de-AI-fication of this website. You will remove generic, templated, low-effort execution and replace it with a specific, coherent, professionally art-directed design and implementation.

You must preserve the site’s business, message, principal visual theme, brand identity, contact details, and working functionality. You are improving how the idea is expressed. You are not replacing the idea.

You must implement, not just advise. Deliverables consisting only of recommendations, written audits, mockups disconnected from the real implementation, or a few cosmetic CSS tweaks are failures. Work in the real site using the access you actually have. If you only have a live site or browser, use only the editing capabilities actually available and say plainly what you could not change. Never claim to have modified, tested, or verified anything you did not.

Define the target correctly. The goal is not to hide AI involvement with arbitrary visual tricks. The goal is demonstrable specificity, originality, coherence, and craftsmanship, at the level a credible professional portfolio piece would show. “Award-caliber” means ambition in concept and precision in execution. It never means sacrificing clarity, usability, or business purpose for spectacle. Do not describe the result as “award-winning.”

Calibrate your own taste. AI tooling has made “polished” the baseline, so polish is no longer a differentiator. Specificity is. The central test throughout is the swap test: if you replaced this site’s logo, name, and accent color with another business’s and nothing else about the design would need to change, the design is still generic. The finished site must fail that swap test, meaning it must be visibly built for this brand.


2. NON-NEGOTIABLE PRESERVATION RULES
A. Preserve the core idea
Before changing anything, determine from the site and any available source material:

What the site is for, who it is meant to reach, and the central message.
What the business actually offers and what distinguishes it.
What a visitor should understand immediately, and what action they should take.
Preserve all of this. Fix presentation problems without changing meaning.

B. Preserve the principal theme and brand identity
Identify the existing design direction before modifying anything: dominant palette, main background treatment (dark/light), brand personality, atmosphere, formality, established brand assets, distinctive motifs, and what makes the site recognizable.

Do not flip a dark site to light, turn a restrained design maximalist, or import an unrelated art direction in the name of originality.
You may substantially refine the execution: typography, composition, surface treatments, supporting colors, gradients, contrast, decorative treatments, and component design.
Distinguish a genuine part of the brand’s identity from a superficial treatment that merely makes the site look like a template. Keep the first, rework the second.
The result must remain recognizable as the same brand, executed at a much higher level.
C. Preserve every contact detail
Before editing, build an inventory of every contact and identity detail on the site, in every location it appears: business or personal name, phone numbers, emails, physical addresses, service areas, social profile links, WhatsApp links, booking links, form destinations, messaging channels, legal business details, and license or registration numbers.

Do not rewrite, substitute, remove, or invent any of them.
Keep them consistent wherever they repeat.
After the redesign, diff the final site against the inventory item by item.
Verify that tel: links, mailto: links, external links, and form submissions still point to the original destinations and work.
If details are contradictory, broken, or unverifiable, preserve the original and flag the problem in your report. Do not silently pick a replacement.
D. Preserve functionality and useful assets
Identify and keep working forms, integrations, analytics, tracking, embeds, booking tools, CMS bindings, navigation destinations, redirects, and necessary configuration. Do not remove a feature because it is invisible in a screenshot. Keep useful content, assets, and interactions that serve the site well. Do not change form submission endpoints or third-party integrations without explicit authorization.

E. Never fabricate
Never invent customers, testimonials, reviews, awards, certifications, statistics, customer counts, guarantees, credentials, case studies, portfolio projects, team members, client logos, locations, product capabilities, or pricing. This explicitly includes “Join 10,000+ satisfied users” style numbers and made-up review snippets. If content is weak, improve its presentation, clarify it, merge it, or remove it. If an essential fact is missing, use genuine existing evidence or clearly labeled development placeholders, and list each placeholder in your final report. Never present generated or stock imagery as real company work.


3. HOW TO TREAT THE RESEARCH-DERIVED PATTERNS
The patterns below come from practitioner commentary and teardowns (designer forums, design-tool anti-pattern lists, and an accessibility audit of an AI-built site). They are heuristics, not controlled studies. Treat them as hypotheses to test against the actual site. Do not turn them into blanket bans.

For each pattern: (1) understand it; (2) look for it on this site and record observable evidence; (3) decide whether it is actually a problem in this context; (4) identify the underlying design or implementation weakness; (5) choose a contextual correction; (6) implement it; (7) verify the result is better.

False-positive rule. Centered heroes, Inter or other common sans-serifs, dark themes, gradients, glassmorphism, card grids, eyebrow labels, logo rows, testimonials, FAQs, and pricing tables are not inherently wrong. They are wrong when used by default, without a reason, identically everywhere, or without real content behind them. If one is doing real work for this brand, keep it and execute it well. Beware replacement clichés: swapping purple gradients for grain-plus-serif-plus-bento-grid, or for brutalist type, is just the next template. Every choice must be justified by this brand’s content and personality.

Pattern catalog
P1. Cookie-cutter page recipe. Centered hero with headline and one bright button, then feature cards, then logo strip or testimonials, then pricing, then FAQ, then CTA, all in uniform full-width sections with symmetric 2/3-column grids.
Inspect: the section sequence, whether each section’s layout is a variation of the same block, whether the order reflects this business’s actual persuasion path or the generic landing-page recipe.
Fix: derive section order and composition from the real story: what this visitor needs to know and trust, in what order. Vary composition only where content or hierarchy justifies it.

P2. “Safe average” spacing and alignment drift. Padding, margins, radii, and alignment shift subtly between sections. Headings are left-aligned in one section and centered in the next. Columns that should match don’t. Some areas are cramped while others are walls of empty space.
Inspect: measure section padding, container widths, gutters, heading alignment, radii, and component heights across the whole page. Look for unintended variation.
Fix: define a spacing scale and container system, apply it deliberately, and allow variation only when it is intentional and legible.

P3. Over-nesting and redundant structure. Cards inside cards, borders around everything, an eyebrow label above every heading, duplicate navigation, redundant hero text, and filler taglines such as “Simple, Transparent Pricing” above a pricing table.
Fix: flatten containers, remove any wrapper that carries no meaning, and keep an eyebrow only where it adds information. Delete taglines that announce a section instead of saying something.

P4. Unconsidered typography. Default system fonts or Inter/Arial used for everything without reason; font families or styles switching mid-page; random words colored, underlined, or italicized for emphasis with no logic; arbitrary bold; inconsistent type scale; skipped heading levels (H1 jumping to H3).
Fix: choose one or two typefaces (at most) for stated reasons tied to the brand voice and readability. Build a consistent scale for size, weight, line-height, tracking, measure, and casing. Use emphasis systematically. Keep heading order semantic (see Domain 14). An established typeface is acceptable if you can justify it and execute it with discipline.

P5. Generic gradient, glow, and shadow vocabulary. Purple/blue/orange gradient blobs behind the hero, dark glows, identical shadow or glow intensity on every element (which flattens depth), pure black or pure gray text and shadows, and default “safe” palettes.
Fix: preserve the principal palette but rebuild it as a system: tinted neutrals rather than pure black or gray, a restrained accent logic, and elevation that varies with function. Keep a gradient or glow only if it earns its place in the brand identity, and then execute it with specificity.

P6. Repetitive, mechanical motion. The same fade-up reveal on every block, identical hover transitions on all buttons and cards, bounce or elastic easing, floating objects, decorative infinite loops, and animation unrelated to interaction.
Fix: audit motion by purpose (see Domain 10). Remove decorative motion, replace default easing with deliberate curves and durations, vary motion only where meaning varies, and honor prefers-reduced-motion.

P7. Generic imagery and iconography. Faceless stock people, laptop flat-lays, abstract shapes, one-size-fits-all icon-library icons (the same shopping-cart or phone glyph on every feature), floating icons, blurred generic avatars, and copy-pasted or repeated fake dashboard or device mockups, sometimes reused across sections.
Fix: prefer real brand and business imagery. Where it is unavailable, use honest, well-art-directed alternatives (typography-led layouts, custom or carefully selected illustration, diagrams grounded in the real offering). Use a single deliberate icon style or remove icons that carry no meaning. Remove duplicate mockups. Never fake interfaces or devices just to fill space.

P8. Placeholder-grade copy and trust signals. Buzzwords (“supercharge,” “effortless,” “powerful,” “streamline”), vague feature bullets, templated CTAs (“Contact us today to get started!”), logo rows and review snippets that feel fabricated, invented statistics, text-only sections with no visual rhythm or concept, and no recognizable brand voice.
Fix: rewrite in a specific, natural voice grounded in verified facts (see Section 8). Show real evidence of trust, or none. Make each CTA say what happens next, in the brand’s voice.

P9. Implementation gaps hidden by a polished surface. Mobile menu truncating options; skipped heading levels; mismatched or hidden alt text; incorrect aria-labels (for example a button labeled differently from its visible action); inert focus traps; small touch targets; no meta tags or weak SEO basics; unoptimized, non-responsive images; static dumps with no sensible content management; no 404 handling; uncached assets; brittle navigation.
Fix: treat these as part of the design brief. Inspect and repair them (see Domains 13 to 15).

P10. Identity gap. The site has no recognizable visual identity, and the design works for almost any business.
Fix: apply the swap test. Introduce brand-specific motifs, compositions, and details that grow from the business’s actual character.


4. PHASE 0: BASELINE INVENTORY (DO THIS BEFORE EDITING)
Inspect the actual site, source files, assets, structure, responsive behavior, and functionality. Produce a structured inventory:

Brand name, identity, core message, offering, target audience, primary visitor action.
Contact and identity details (Section 2C), with every location where each appears.
Navigation structure and destinations (header, footer, in-page anchors, mobile menu).
Principal theme: palette values, background treatment, typography currently used, motifs, mood.
Essential copy that must be retained or only lightly edited.
All assets: images, icons, logos, illustrations, videos, fonts, with notes on quality and authenticity.
Functional elements: forms (and their destinations), embeds, chat widgets, booking tools, analytics and tracking, integrations, scripts.
Existing strengths worth keeping.
Suspected defects, each with evidence (screenshot, selector, line, measurement).
Unverified or contradictory information.
Classify every significant element as Preserve / Refine / Redesign / Remove / Replace / Verify. Keep this classification current as you work. You will audit against it at the end.


5. PHASE B: CREATIVE DIRECTION (REQUIRED BEFORE MAJOR IMPLEMENTATION)
A collection of individually attractive sections is insufficient. Derive a single coherent creative direction from the actual site and brand. Determine:

What is already distinctive about this site.
Which elements make it resemble a generic template.
Which original elements to preserve, and which can be substantially redesigned.
The governing visual principles: how typography, composition, color, imagery, motion, and interaction reinforce one another.
What brand-specific motifs or devices could recur (a structural logic, a type treatment, a recurring crop, a layout rhythm, a detail drawn from the business’s real work or materials).
How the site becomes more memorable without becoming harder to use.
Consider a small number of genuine alternatives, choose one, and state your rationale in a few lines. Do not spiral into endless concepting. Decide, implement, verify. The site’s individuality must come from the relationships between decisions, not from stacking fashionable elements chosen independently.

A restrained design can be exceptional. An ambitious one can also be exceptional. Choose according to the brand, audience, and content.


6. AUDIT AND REDESIGN DOMAINS (EXAMINE ALL; MODIFY ONLY WHAT IS JUSTIFIED)
For each domain: inspect thoroughly, record evidence, decide, implement, verify.

Creative direction and visual personality. Cohesive point of view, hierarchy of attention, use of negative space, whether sections look individually designed or mechanically assembled, and whether memorable characteristics belong to this brand.
Whole-page composition. Header to footer: section order, heights, container widths, alignment, density, grids, background changes, transitions, repeated compositions. Is there real visual rhythm, or just alternation between predictable templates? Do not force uniformity, and do not randomize variation. Every compositional change must serve content, hierarchy, or story.
Hero and first impression. Evaluate headline, supporting copy, CTA hierarchy, typography, visual asset, layout, nav relationship, and above-the-fold composition. Communicate the real purpose clearly and create a distinctive first impression. Clarity outranks spectacle.
Typography. Fonts, weights, scale, line-height, tracking, text measure, line breaks, paragraph density, heading hierarchy, casing, alignment, responsive behavior. Each choice needs a rationale. No unusual fonts just to look original. No arbitrary oversized headings. Fix orphans, awkward wraps, and inconsistent emphasis.
Color and surfaces. Backgrounds, foregrounds, accents, borders, gradients, shadows, translucency, glows, contrast, interactive states. Preserve the principal theme and rebuild the system. Remove arbitrary or formulaic effects. Keep any gradient, glow, glass, or shadow only where it contributes to identity or hierarchy.
Layout primitives and component repetition. Cards, grids, buttons, icon containers, bordered surfaces, stat blocks, feature lists, testimonial layouts, tabs, accordions. Find monotony caused by repetition (for example three identical cards, repeated icon-in-circle, identical sections stacked). Rethink components and the combinations in which they appear. Keep a consistent system without making the page repetitive.
Imagery and visual assets. Audit every image, illustration, mockup, icon, background, and decorative graphic for relevance, authenticity, quality, crop, perspective, lighting, grading, proportions, repetition, and context. Replace or remove weak assets. Prefer assets tied to the real business. No arbitrary 3D objects, abstract fillers, fake UI, or device mockups. Any new assets must follow the creative direction and be honestly sourced or created with the tools you actually have.
Content and information quality. See Section 8.
Buttons, icons, micro-details. Shape, radius, padding, alignment, icon size and weight, borders, label placement, and hover, focus, active, and disabled states. Components should look intentionally built. Avoid both arbitrary inconsistency and robotic sameness.
Motion. Audit page-load motion, hero animation, scroll reveals, hover effects, button feedback, section transitions, background motion, floating objects, parallax, infinite loops, marquees, animated text, cursor effects, loaders, modal and menu transitions, timing, duration, easing, stagger, real-use performance, and reduced-motion behavior. Remove motion that is decorative, repetitive, mechanical, or unrelated to the interaction. Keep useful motion and refine it. Add new motion only with a clear purpose. The site must not feel like an animation showcase.
Navigation and information architecture. Header layout, labels, grouping, dropdowns, sticky behavior, destinations, mobile menu, footer navigation. Improve clarity without breaking or removing needed pages.
Forms, conversion, and functional controls. Audit presentation and behavior of contact forms, quote requests, CTAs, messaging links, booking controls, and chat widgets. No decorative buttons that do nothing. No mockup forms replacing working ones. Every visible control must have a verified purpose.
Responsive design. Inspect large desktop, laptop, tablet, mobile, narrow mobile, and intermediate breakpoints where layouts transition. Check composition, type, image crops, spacing, navigation (menus must not truncate options), content order, density, overflow, touch targets, sticky elements, and CTA visibility. Mobile must feel designed for mobile, not like a squeezed desktop.
Accessibility and usability. Contrast, semantic structure, correct heading order (no skipped levels), landmarks, accurate accessible names and aria-labels that match visible actions, meaningful alt text (and no hidden misleading alt), form labels, keyboard operation, visible focus indicators, focus management in menus and modals (no traps), touch target size, reduced motion, and relevant WCAG criteria. Polish never excuses unusable design.
Technical implementation. Do not assume a framework. Inspect the real stack before making framework-specific changes. Check broken links, missing assets, 404 handling, console errors, layout shift, rendering inconsistencies, unnecessary dependencies, unoptimized or non-responsive images (proper sizing, formats, lazy loading), caching, meta tags, titles, descriptions, social/Open Graph tags, structured data where relevant, and maintainability. Where content management exists, preserve and respect it.
Design system and consistency. Build or refine tokens for type, spacing, color, borders, radii, shadows, controls, layout, and breakpoints. Equivalent components behave and look equivalent. Differences exist only for clear design reasons.
Footer and secondary content. The footer, legal text, lower sections, and minor pages get the same care as the hero. A sophisticated hero must not mask a templated remainder.
Pixel-level finishing. Optical alignment, line breaks, component heights, inconsistent borders, subtle overlaps, clipping, image positioning, small responsive defects, and accumulated spacing differences. Review the whole experience, not a handful of flattering screenshots.

7. ORIGINALITY WITHOUT GIMMICKS
Seek distinctiveness in composition, hierarchy, typography, use of space, brand-specific motifs, image selection, content presentation, section transitions, navigation, motion, component relationships, subtle detail, and the overall feel of scrolling the page.

Do not pursue it through random asymmetry, excess animation, odd fonts, illegible type, unnecessary interactive effects, or decorative complexity. Complexity is not premium. The result should look intentionally designed, not like it is visibly trying to prove it was not generated.


8. CONTENT AUDIT: REMOVE USELESS CONTENT INTELLIGENTLY
For every section and content block, answer:

Does it communicate something useful?
Does it support the site’s real purpose?
Does it help visitors understand, evaluate, trust, or contact the business?
Does it repeat something stated elsewhere?
Would removing it improve or damage the experience?
Is it accurate and verifiable?
Is its visual weight proportionate to its importance?
Remove filler, redundant claims, repeated CTAs, section-announcing taglines, meaningless statistics, empty buzzwords, repeated section intros, placeholder bullets, and decorative content that adds length without value.
Rewrite weak content into specific, natural, plain-spoken copy grounded in verified facts and the brand’s existing voice. Make service descriptions concrete: what is done, for whom, and what the visitor does next.
Replace low-value blocks with stronger material only where verified facts exist in the site or authorized source material.
Do not add testimonials, stats, pricing, case studies, team bios, or claims without a factual basis, and do not remove a section merely because it is common elsewhere. Do not rewrite good copy just because the wording is unfashionable.

The page may become shorter or longer only where that better serves the real site.


9. ANTI-OVERDESIGN RULE
“De-AI-fy everything” does not mean “change everything.” Inspection must be exhaustive; modification must be proportional to diagnosed problems. Some elements may already be excellent, some need small refinements, some need redesign. Do not let the site become chaotic, over-animated, needlessly asymmetric, loaded with bespoke effects, or hard to understand. Consistency must not become monotony, and originality must not become incoherence. Preserve quality, remove carelessness, add specificity, eliminate unnecessary complexity.


10. WORKFLOW (FOLLOW IN ORDER)
A. Inspect. Examine the live site, source, assets, structure, responsive layouts, and behavior. Record specific issues with evidence.
B. Establish direction. Document strengths, failures, and a coherent creative direction that preserves the principal theme. Prioritize changes by their effect on the whole experience.
C. Plan. Write a concise plan covering the whole site, not whichever section is most tempting.
D. Implement. Make substantive changes in the real project: hierarchy, components, typography, spacing, content presentation, assets, motion, responsive layouts, technical fixes.
E. Inspect the real result. Run or open the revised site. Review full pages and individual sections with screenshots, browser inspection, source inspection, and testing tools where available. Do not rely on your mental picture of what the code renders.
F. Repair. Fix clipping, overflow, spacing inconsistencies, awkward wrapping, broken functionality, regressions, responsive faults, and loading problems, including those you introduced.
G. Verify. Recheck the baseline inventory, contact details, interactions, responsiveness, visual consistency, and accessibility.
H. Finalize. Deliver the implemented site and an honest report (Section 13).


11. TESTING REQUIREMENTS
Do not declare success after viewing a desktop hero screenshot. Review full pages at large desktop, standard laptop, tablet, mobile, narrow mobile, and any breakpoint where layout changes. Check real text wrapping and layout behavior rather than trusting media queries. Test the interactions that actually exist on this site, not an imaginary checklist: main navigation, buttons, contact links, forms, menus (including that no options are cut off on mobile), accordions, modals, chat elements, scroll-triggered behavior, hover and focus states, keyboard navigation, and reduced-motion settings. Where tools exist, run an accessibility scan, link check, and performance or Lighthouse-style check, and read the console. Record what you actually ran and what you could not.


12. FINAL QUALITY GATE (DO NOT DECLARE DONE UNTIL IT PASSES)
Compare the result against the Phase 0 baseline and answer each question honestly.

Identity: Is the core idea intact? Is the principal theme still recognizable? Is the business identity preserved?
Contact and information integrity: Is every original contact detail retained and correct? Do tel:, mailto:, external links, and form destinations work as before? Has anything been fabricated or changed without authorization?
Visual quality: Is there one coherent direction? Have the patterns in Section 3 been addressed where they actually existed? Is typography controlled? Are spacing and alignment consistent? Are assets relevant and credible? Are effects intentional? Does the footer match the hero in polish? Did I swap one cliché for another?
Content: Is useless and repetitive content gone? Is remaining content specific and useful? Are all claims supported? Does each section have an identifiable purpose?
Function: Does every visible control do what it advertises? Do forms submit to the right place? Are integrations intact?
Responsive: Does it look intentional at every width? Any clipping, overflow, awkward wrapping, truncated menus, or unusable CTAs?
Technical and accessibility: Heading order correct? Accessible names accurate? Alt text meaningful? Focus visible and not trapped? Contrast adequate? Images sized and optimized? Meta tags and 404 handling in place? Console clean? Which tests were actually run?
Distinctiveness: Does it still read as a template? Does it pass the swap test? Are the strongest decisions specific to this brand? Do the sections form a coherent whole?

If an important check fails, fix it where your capabilities allow and re-verify the relevant area.


13. REQUIRED FINAL REPORT
Deliver the implemented site plus a concise, honest report containing:

The creative direction chosen and why.
What was preserved (with the contact-detail diff result).
What was refined, redesigned, removed, and replaced, and why.
Which Section 3 patterns were found on this site, and which were checked and judged acceptable in context.
Content removed or rewritten, and any development placeholders still present.
Tests actually performed (viewports, interactions, accessibility, performance) and their results.
Unresolved issues, unverifiable information, contradictions found in the original, and capability limits that blocked work.
Never claim a test, fix, or verification that did not happen.


14. PROHIBITED FAILURE MODES
Delivering only advice, an audit, or a mockup.
Superficial changes (a font swap, removing one gradient, new border radii, recolored cards) presented as de-AI-fication.
Changing the brand’s identity, theme direction, or business meaning.
Removing, altering, or inventing contact details, links, or form destinations.
Fabricating proof, statistics, testimonials, logos, or claims.
Breaking working functionality or removing integrations.
Replacing AI clichés with different clichés.
Adding complexity, animation, or asymmetry as proof of effort.
Treating research-derived patterns as absolute bans.
Polishing the hero while leaving the rest and the footer generic.
Declaring success from a desktop screenshot or from code you never rendered.
Overstating what you tested.
 
 
 
 
 
 